# Source manifest — reproducible-by-reference (Invariant 3).
# Re-clone at build time; the working tree under sources/codex/ is gitignored.
#
# TOOLCHAIN SOURCE — a tool this repo actually RUNS (#81, ingested 2026-08-02).
#
# ⚠️ INVARIANT: this pin MUST track the version we actually run.
#   version source: mise.toml `"npm:@openai/codex"` (line ~232)
#   pinned here:    see `ref` below — this comment used to restate the number and
#                   drifted from it (it read 0.152.0 while `ref` said
#                   rust-v0.152.1). A version written twice in one file is a
#                   version that will disagree with itself; `ref` is the value.
# Note the tag prefix: releases are `rust-v<version>`, so `ref` is NOT the bare
# version the two lines above name. Also: this manifest read 0.146.0 while
# mise.toml ran 0.146.1, i.e. it was ALREADY drifting before the 0.147.0 bump —
# back then the invariant above was stated in 13 manifests and enforced by
# nothing.
#
# THAT IS NO LONGER TRUE, and this comment claimed otherwise until 2026-09-03.
# It read: "`currency.toml`'s `[tool.codex]` block deliberately carries no
# `manifest` key — so this pin is advanced BY HAND and unenforced by
# `kb-currency-check`". `currency.toml` DOES declare
# `manifest = "sources/codex.manifest"`, so the check runs and this pin is
# enforced. The comment was worse than merely stale: it told a reader that
# nothing would catch them, which is an invitation to hand-edit carelessly.
# A pin that drifts from the installed version makes the corpus answer questions
# about code we are not running. Not hypothetical — `sources/graphify.manifest`
# records exactly that failure: it sat two releases behind for weeks, so every
# "how does graphify work?" answer came from the wrong source.
#
# Pin the RELEASE TAG, never a branch head — a branch drifts ahead of the release.
# ⚠️ THIS TAG IS ANNOTATED. Do NOT use `--refs` — it strips the peeled line,
# and a bump recorded from it pins the TAG OBJECT, not the commit `kb-build`
# checks out (#500). To bump: change the version in its source above, then
#   git ls-remote --tags https://github.com/openai/codex <version> '<version>^{}'
# and set ref/commit here to the `^{}` (peeled) line's SHA — NOT the bare
# `<version>` line — then `mise run kb-build`.
#
# ⚠️⚠️ PASS BOTH PATTERNS, or omit the pattern entirely. `--refs` is not the only
# way to lose the peeled line: a SINGLE bare pattern hides it just as silently.
# Measured 2026-09-08 while bumping to 0.153.4 —
#   `git ls-remote --tags <url> rust-v0.153.4`      -> ONE line, no `^{}`
#   `git ls-remote --tags <url> | grep rust-v0.153` -> TWO lines, `^{}` present
# The one-line output looks exactly like a lightweight tag, so it reads as "there
# is no peeled line here" rather than "you filtered it out", and recording it
# would have pinned `042fb41b…` (the tag object) instead of `3d2ee51c…` (the
# commit). The control arm is this manifest's own previous pin: 0.153.1's bare
# line is `f5c2c463…` while its `^{}` line is `98564127…`, the value that was
# committed here — proving the peeled line existed all along.
#
# kind = code: AST extraction only, no LLM, no cost. NOTE this extracts the
# SOURCE and skips every .md by design — the tool's DOCS are a separate
# `kind = docs` mirror (#123), not this manifest.
url = https://github.com/openai/codex
ref = rust-v0.154.0
commit = 6b9826e3aa83b1a5947db50f4332cb9c65f1b340
kind = code
added = 2026-08-02
# INCLUDED IN THE BUILD AGAIN, 2026-09-09 (#728 step 4). It was `build = skip`
# from 2026-08-20 under Ray's "skip any blocker, file it, triage later" ruling,
# tracked in #417. The skip_reason claimed `kb-build` fails closed on ONE file —
# `codex-rs/Cargo.toml` producing zero nodes (#1666) — and that approving it was
# impossible because "the reviewed-metadata approver is JSON-only, so a TOML file
# needs new machinery".
#
# BOTH HALVES OF THAT ARE REFUTED at the installed graphify 0.9.57, read from the
# source rather than from the issue tracker:
#   * `manifest_ingest.py` gives `Cargo.toml` its OWN first-class parser, routed
#     ahead of the JSON extractor by `extract.py:5933-5934`. TOML has had a
#     dedicated path since #1377, predating this skip.
#   * `manifest_ingest.py:94-96` returns `{"nodes": [], "edges": []}` for a
#     manifest with no name. A Rust virtual `[workspace]` root genuinely has no
#     package to emit, so zero nodes is a CORRECT parse, not data loss.
#
# No graphify fork change was needed. What was missing was one registry entry, of
# the exact shape eight sibling sources already carry (uv, ruff, biome, typos,
# taplo, logbook, picologging, pensyve): see
# `graph.py`'s `_EXPECTED_METADATA_ONLY`, `source_name="codex"`.
#
# ⚠️ THE ENTRY IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT BOOKKEEPING. `require_complete`
# (`graphify_health.py:528`) fails a build CLOSED on any zero-node file that is
# not pre-registered — so deleting these lines WITHOUT that entry swaps a skipped
# source for a failing build. PR #550 is the precedent: it let a different skip
# through and died on `ast-grep`'s Cargo.toml warning.
#
# The invariant at the top of this file binds again in the direction it was
# written for: this is a TOOLCHAIN source, and the corpus can now describe the
# codex we actually run.
